Node.js, or Node, is an asynchronous server environment that uses [[JavaScript]].

Why do we need server side? Well, because some things can *only* happen on a server. For example user authentication. 

While there are many older server environment (php, asp, ruby on rails, etc), none of these older technologies are [[Asynchronous JS|asynchronous]]. 

As it turns out, asynchronousity (not a word lol), on the server side has some key advantages.
ex:
- code can be *non-blocking*
```php
$connection = new PDO
$result1 = $connection->query
$result2 = $connection->query
```
In php these threads are blocked. As a consequence, most PHP sites need a lot of servers.

A typical linux server can handle ~2000 requests/threads.

Blocking code exacerbates this problem .

Node can be written in a non-blocking way, which is done by using the JS event architecture.
```js
database.connect(connString, rejectFn, reactiveFn)
```

***Pros and cons***
**Pros**
- Non-blocking architecture allows a single CPU to handle more requests.
- JS is just everywhere, so from an HR standpoint it makes sense to use node.
- Push architecture possible, in the web space this uses the [[WebSocket Protocol]]
- Very rich ecosystem of code. Node makes use of a package called [[NPM]] which has a bunch of packages available. ex: `npm install expression` via command line, which will install a [[JSON]] package with dependencies and [[JavaScript]] code.
- Broadly adopted, node is super popular 
- [[Cloud]] integration: most cloud based environments use node.
- can be used to create server software analogous to [[Apache]]. That is great for working with [[HTTP]] header.
**Cons**
- Async coding can be hard to debug and write.
- Not ideal for traditional dynamic content sites. Environments such as [[PHP]] or [[ASP.NET]] do this easier 
*NOTE* Node and CLI (command line interface) 

Example:
`simple-server.js`
```js
const http = require("https");
const server = http.createServer( (request, response) => {
//invoked in the future when an http request is receive
			console.log(`request from ${request.url}`);
			response.writeHead(200, {"Content" : "text/plain"});
			//200 is the http status code
			response.write("hello world");
			response.end();
});
server.listen(8080); //Listen to specified port for http
```
***IMPORTANT*** - Node has been around since 2009. It needed a mechanism for [[Encapsulation]]. That is, code in one package doesn't interfere with other packages. Thats where the
[[CommonJS Module]] system comes in. In the code above, `require("http")` http is the module name. Require returns *something.*

***IMPORTANT-ER*** - All modules (excluding build in ones) need to be installed via [[NPM]].

***EVEN IMPORTANT-ER*** - `Require()` returns *something*, typically an object

```bash
$ node simple-server
>> server running on port 8080
```

Now it's just waiting for a request. So on the browser:
`http://localhost:8080`
Then in CLI,
```bash
>> request from ...
```
Then hello world shows up in browser.

***SO FUCKING IMPORTANT*** - 
```js
response.writeHead(200, {"Content" : "text/plain"});
response.write("<html><body><h1>Hello World</h1></body></html>");
```
You would typically make use of some [[API]] rather than doing this, for example [[Express]].

